The postmortem begins with a captured response from the fictional site example.test. A page returned a normal status code, loaded its shared header, and then navigated the browser to a second host. The engineer reproduced the behavior only inside a local copy. The minimal vulnerable pattern in the fixture was:

const next = new URL(new URLSearchParams(location.search).get("next"), location.href);
window.location.assign(next.href);

The defect was not the browser API by itself; it was accepting an attacker-controlled destination without an origin or path policy. In the real incident, the same logic had been inserted into a shared template, so a single altered asset affected many routes. A compromised tag manager or advertising script could have produced a similar symptom, which is why the team compared the deployed bundle with the signed build rather than assuming the page template was the only cause.

For a safe test, the engineer replaced the fixture with an explicit local allowlist:

const allowed = new Set(["/lab/ok", "/lab/cancel"]);
const raw = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get("next") || "/lab/ok";
const candidate = new URL(raw, location.origin);
if (candidate.origin !== location.origin || !allowed.has(candidate.pathname)) {
  throw new Error("blocked test destination");
}
window.location.assign(candidate.href);

The test ran on localhost with mock pages and no external network access. Cases covered an external HTTPS URL, a protocol-relative URL, an encoded separator, and a javascript scheme; each was rejected. The engineer also tested a server-side redirect handler because a browser fix cannot repair a server response that sends a 3xx location.

Containment included removing the altered asset, restoring a verified build, revoking publishing credentials, invalidating caches, reviewing service workers, and preserving the original files and logs. The final report recommends signed deployments, two-person approval, content-security-policy reporting, subresource integrity, and allowlisted redirect destinations. The code excerpt makes the incident reproducible in a lab while the remediation explains why the same behavior should not be deployed against a real site.
